# Git Workflow

Collaboration policy for {{.ProjectName}}: how branches, commits, pull
requests, reviews, merges, releases, and CI gates work. This guide is the
canonical source for the detailed workflow — `AGENTS.md` only points here.

## Branching

- **GitHub Flow.** `main` is always releasable. All work happens on
  short-lived branches cut from an up-to-date `main`.
- **Branch names** follow `<type>/<short-topic>`, e.g. `feat/login-form`,
  `fix/null-deref`, `docs/readme-install`. Use the Conventional Commits
  type as the prefix; keep the topic in kebab-case.
- Delete the branch after merge. Long-lived branches are avoided; re-cut
  from `main` rather than letting a branch drift.

## Commits

- **Conventional Commits.** Each commit subject is
  `<type>(<scope>)?: <summary>`. Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`,
  `style`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`, `perf`, `ci`, `build`.
- Subject in the imperative mood, no trailing period, ~72 chars max.
- Describe the change, not the tooling. Do not add AI / assistant
  signatures to commit messages.

## Pull requests

- **Small PRs.** Prefer one logical change per PR; split unrelated work.
  A reviewer should be able to read the whole diff in one sitting.
- Every change lands through a PR; do not push directly to `main`.
- The PR description states what changed and why, and links any related
  issue or ADR.
- **Squash-only merge.** A PR merges as a single squashed commit whose
  subject follows Conventional Commits, keeping `main` history linear and
  one-commit-per-change.

## Review and merge

- At least one approving review before merge. Where the project allows it,
  a trusted committer may self-merge once CI is green.
- All required CI checks must pass before merge — see below.
- Resolve review threads before merging; never merge over an unresolved
  blocking comment.

## Releases

- **SemVer tags.** Releases are tagged `vMAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`
  (e.g. `v1.4.0`): MAJOR for breaking changes, MINOR for
  backward-compatible features, PATCH for fixes.
- Tag from `main` at a commit where CI is green.
- Where a project ships mobile apps or needs to stabilise a release, cut a
  short-lived `release/<version>` branch for final fixes and tag from
  there. Urgent production fixes use a `hotfix/<topic>` branch merged back
  to `main`.

## CI gates

- Required checks (build, lint, tests, and any project-specific gates)
  must be green before a PR can merge. Treat a red gate as a blocker, not
  a suggestion.
- Keep `main` green: if a merge breaks CI, fixing or reverting it takes
  priority over new work.
